    "plan_strategy": "Single attempt: replace the linear baseline with a gradient-boosted tree ensemble (hist_gbdt) fitted on all 57 numeric columns, tuned for a strong ranking rather than for an operating point. Spambase is a classic case where axis-aligned tree splits on highly skewed word/char frequency counts (thresholds like word_freq_remove > 0.05, char_freq_%24 > 0.05, capital_run_length_longest > 30) capture non-linear, interaction-heavy structure that logistic regression on standardised features cannot express — which is exactly the ranking improvement the goal demands, since the baseline's own ranking ceiling (0.9415) sits below the bar. Configuration: hist_gbdt with max_iter=500, learning_rate=0.06, max_leaf_nodes=31, max_depth=None (leaf-limited growth), min_samples_leaf=15, l2_regularization=1.0, early_stopping=True with validation_fraction=0.1 and n_iter_no_change=30 so the iteration count is chosen by the executor's internal held-out slice rather than fixed by guess. Leave the operating point alone: no class_weight, because at 1.54:1 imbalance the default 0.5 cut on a well-ranked GBDT is already near-symmetric in recall/specificity, and balanced_accuracy on this dataset is limited by ranking quality, not by where the cut falls. Preprocessing: median impute (there is no missingness, so this is a no-op) and scale=false — hist_gbdt is scale-invariant, so standardisation buys nothing and only adds a fitted transform. No missingness columns: overall missing rate is 0.0, so missing_indicator would be constant and missing_count identically zero. Read on return: roc_auc and pr_auc against the baseline's 0.9772/0.9647 to confirm the ranking actually moved, balanced_accuracy_at_best_cut against 0.9415 to confirm the ceiling rose, cut_headroom to confirm the default cut is near-optimal, and train_val_gap plus train_balanced_accuracy for overfitting."
